# Parsing an `Event` from `unknown`

The key idea: never trust `unknown` — narrow it with runtime checks before returning a typed value.

```ts
// ---- Shape ----
export interface Event {
  name: string;
  timestamp: number; // epoch ms
  payload: Record<string, unknown>;
}

// ---- Error type (optional but useful) ----
export class EventParseError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string, readonly cause?: unknown) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "EventParseError";
  }
}

// ---- Type guards ----
function isRecord(v: unknown): v is Record<string, unknown> {
  return typeof v === "object" && v !== null && !Array.isArray(v);
}

// ---- Parser ----
export function parseEvent(raw: unknown): Event {
  // Accept either an object or a JSON string.
  let value: unknown = raw;
  if (typeof raw === "string") {
    try {
      value = JSON.parse(raw);
    } catch (err) {
      throw new EventParseError("Event is not valid JSON", err);
    }
  }

  if (!isRecord(value)) {
    throw new EventParseError("Event must be an object");
  }

  const { name, timestamp, payload } = value;

  if (typeof name !== "string" || name.length === 0) {
    throw new EventParseError("Event.name must be a non-empty string");
  }

  if (typeof timestamp !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(timestamp)) {
    throw new EventParseError("Event.timestamp must be a finite number");
  }

  if (!isRecord(payload)) {
    throw new EventParseError("Event.payload must be an object");
  }

  return { name, timestamp, payload };
}
```

## Usage

```ts
const e = parseEvent('{"name":"user.created","timestamp":1717000000000,"payload":{"id":42}}');
// e: Event
// { name: "user.created", timestamp: 1717000000000, payload: { id: 42 } }

parseEvent({ name: "x", timestamp: NaN, payload: {} });
// throws EventParseError: Event.timestamp must be a finite number
```

## Notes / variations

- **Stricter payload typing.** If you know the payload shape per event name, use a discriminated union instead of `Record<string, unknown>`:

  ```ts
  type Event =
    | { name: "user.created"; timestamp: number; payload: { id: number } }
    | { name: "user.deleted"; timestamp: number; payload: { id: number; reason: string } };
  ```

- **Schema libraries.** For anything non-trivial, prefer [Zod](https://zod.dev), [Valibot](https://valibot.dev), or [ArkType](https://arktype.io) — they generate the type *and* the validator from one source:

  ```ts
  import { z } from "zod";
  const EventSchema = z.object({
    name: z.string().min(1),
    timestamp: z.number().finite(),
    payload: z.record(z.unknown()),
  });
  export type Event = z.infer<typeof EventSchema>;
  export const parseEvent = (raw: unknown): Event => EventSchema.parse(raw);
  ```

- **Why not `as Event`?** A cast silences the compiler but does nothing at runtime — malformed input would flow through as if it were valid. The guards above are the minimum needed to make the return type honest.

```ts
