The key idea: keep the `Event` type as the single source of truth, and write a parser that narrows `unknown` into it with runtime checks. TypeScript's type system is erased at runtime, so we need explicit guards.

```ts
// ---- The shape ----------------------------------------------------------

export interface Event {
  name: string;
  timestamp: number; // epoch millis
  payload: Record<string, unknown>;
}

// ---- Runtime guards -----------------------------------------------------

function isRecord(value: unknown): value is Record<string, unknown> {
  return typeof value === "object" && value !== null && !Array.isArray(value);
}

// ---- Parser -------------------------------------------------------------

export function parseEvent(raw: unknown): Event {
  if (!isRecord(raw)) {
    throw new TypeError("Event must be an object");
  }

  const { name, timestamp, payload } = raw;

  if (typeof name !== "string" || name.length === 0) {
    throw new TypeError("Event.name must be a non-empty string");
  }

  if (typeof timestamp !== "number" || !Number.isFinite(timestamp)) {
    throw new TypeError("Event.timestamp must be a finite number");
  }

  if (!isRecord(payload)) {
    throw new TypeError("Event.payload must be an object");
  }

  return { name, timestamp, payload };
}
```

## Why this shape

- **`unknown` in, `Event` out.** The function signature forces callers to deal with the possibility of bad data, and the return type is fully narrowed.
- **`isRecord` guard.** `typeof x === "object"` alone is not enough — `null` and arrays also pass that check. The guard excludes both.
- **`Number.isFinite`.** Rejects `NaN` and `Infinity`, which are technically `number` but almost never valid timestamps.
- **`payload: Record<string, unknown>`.** Keeps the payload open-ended (any JSON-ish object) while still being safe to index into. If you know the payload schema, make `Event` generic: `Event<T> = { name: string; timestamp: number; payload: T }`.

## Usage

```ts
