# TypeScript `fetch` Wrapper with `RequestOptions`

```typescript
/**
 * Options for making an HTTP request.
 */
export interface RequestOptions {
  /** HTTP method (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.). Defaults to "GET". */
  method?: string;

  /** The URL to send the request to. */
  url: string;

  /** Optional headers to include with the request. */
  headers?: Record<string, string>;

  /** Optional request body. Can be a string, plain object, FormData, etc. */
  body?: BodyInit | Record<string, unknown> | null;
}

/**
 * Wrapper around `fetch` that accepts a `RequestOptions` object.
 *
 * @param options - The request configuration.
 * @returns A promise resolving to the `Response` from `fetch`.
 */
export function request(options: RequestOptions): Promise<Response> {
  const { method = "GET", url, headers = {}, body } = options;

  // Prepare the body: serialize plain objects to JSON, otherwise pass through.
  let processedBody: BodyInit | undefined;
  const finalHeaders: Record<string, string> = { ...headers };

  if (body !== undefined && body !== null) {
    if (
      typeof body === "object" &&
      !(body instanceof FormData) &&
      !(body instanceof Blob) &&
      !(body instanceof URLSearchParams) &&
      !(body instanceof ArrayBuffer) &&
      !ArrayBuffer.isView(body)
    ) {
      processedBody = JSON.stringify(body);
      if (!finalHeaders["Content-Type"]) {
        finalHeaders["Content-Type"] = "application/json";
      }
    } else {
      processedBody = body as BodyInit;
    }
  }

  return fetch(url, {
    method,
    headers: finalHeaders,
    body: processedBody,
  });
}
```

## Usage

```typescript
// Simple GET
const res = await request({ url: "https://api.example.com/users" });

// POST with JSON body (auto-serialized + Content-Type set)
const created = await request({
  method: "POST",
  url: "https://api.example.com/users",
  headers: { Authorization: "Bearer token" },
  body: { name: "Alice", age: 30 },
});

// POST with FormData (passed through untouched)
const form = new FormData();
form.append("file", fileInput.files![0]);
await request({ method: "POST", url: "/upload", body: form });
```

## Notes

- **`BodyInit`** is a built-in DOM type covering `string`, `Blob`, `FormData`, `URLSearchParams`, `ArrayBuffer`, typed arrays, and `ReadableStream`.
- The union `BodyInit | Record<string, unknown>` lets callers pass a plain object and have it auto-JSON-serialized.
- `method` defaults to `"GET"` and `headers` defaults to `{}` via destructuring defaults.
- If you need typed responses, you can generalize the function: `request<T>(options: RequestOptions): Promise<T>` and `return res.json() as Promise<T>` — but the version above returns the raw `Response`, which is the most flexible base primitive.

