`HeadersInit` and `BodyInit` are built-in DOM types, so make sure your `tsconfig.json` includes the DOM library, e.g. `"lib": ["DOM", "ES2020"]`.

```typescript
export interface RequestOptions {
  method: 'GET' | 'POST' | 'PUT' | 'PATCH' | 'DELETE';
  url: string;
  headers?: Record<string, string>;
  body?: unknown;
}

export function request(
  options: RequestOptions,
  signal?: AbortSignal,
): Promise<Response> {
  const { method, url, headers = {}, body } = options;

  const init: RequestInit = {
    method,
    headers: { ...headers },
    signal,
  };

  // Attach a body only when one was supplied.
  // Objects/arrays are JSON-encoded automatically; strings pass through.
  if (body !== undefined && method !== 'GET') {
    if (typeof body === 'string' || body instanceof FormData || body instanceof Blob) {
      init.body = body as BodyInit;
    } else {
      init.body = JSON.stringify(body);
      (init.headers as Record<string, string>)['Content-Type'] ??= 'application/json';
    }
  }

  return fetch(url, init);
}
```

### Usage

```typescript
const res = await request({
  method: 'POST',
  url: 'https://api.example.com/users',
  headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer abc' },
  body: { name: 'Alice' },
});

const data = await res.json();
```

### Notes

1. **Returns a raw `Promise<Response>`** — callers decide whether to `.json()`, `.text()`, or stream it. If you'd rather have a typed, throw-on-error helper, wrap it:

   ```typescript
   export async function requestJson<T>(options: RequestOptions): Promise<T> {
     const res = await request(options);
     if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status} ${res.statusText}`);
     return res.json() as Promise<T>;
   }
   ```

2. **Name collision:** lib.dom.d.ts already declares a `RequestOptions` type. If you're in a DOM-enabled project and don't want them to merge, rename yours to `ApiRequestOptions` (or put it in a module/namespace).

3. **`Content-Type` guard:** the `??=` ensures a caller-supplied `Content-Type` isn't overwritten when auto-serializing an object body.

```typescript
interface RequestOptions {
  method: string;
  url: string;
  headers?: Record<string, string>;
  body?: unknown;
}

async function request<T = unknown>(options: RequestOptions): Promise<T> {
  const { method, url, headers, body } = options;

  const init: RequestInit = {
    method,
    headers: {
      'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      ...headers,
    },
  };

  if (body !== undefined && method.toUpperCase() !== 'GET' && method.toUpperCase() !== 'HEAD') {
    init.body = typeof body === 'string' ? body : JSON.stringify(body);
  }

  const response = await fetch(url, init);

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
  }

  const contentType = response.headers.get('content-type') ?? '';
  if (contentType.includes('application/json')) {
    return (await response.json()) as T;
  }
  return (await response.text()) as unknown as T;
}
```

Key points:

- **`RequestOptions`** declares `method`, `url`, optional `headers`, and optional `body`.
- **`body` is `unknown`** so callers can pass an object, string, `FormData`, etc. Objects are `JSON.stringify`'d; strings pass through.
- **GET/HEAD guard** — the Fetch spec forbids a body on those methods, so we skip setting it.
- **Generic `<T>`** lets callers type the parsed response: `const user = await request<User>({...})`.
- **Error on non-2xx** by throwing — you can also return `response` if you'd rather let callers inspect the status.
- **Content-type sniffing** decides between `.json()` and `.text()`.

