## Why this shape

- **`raw: unknown`** forces callers to prove their data is valid; `any` would silently disable checking.
- **Types are erased at runtime**, so the `isRecord`/field checks are what actually enforce the `Event` contract — the declared interface alone guarantees nothing about JSON coming off the wire.
- **Narrowing via `isRecord`** is what lets TypeScript let you read `raw.name` etc. after the guard, instead of casting with `as`.
- **`payload: Record<string, unknown>`** keeps the payload open-ended while still type-safe; if you know the payload shape per event name, model it as a discriminated union on `name` and narrow with a switch.
- **`timestamp` normalized to epoch ms** avoids leaking two representations of the same instant into your codebase; store the raw value separately if you need it.

One thing to decide for your domain: whether `timestamp` should be `number` (epoch ms) or a `Date`. `number` is simpler to serialize and compare; `Date` is friendlier to read but a common source of timezone bugs. The above picks `number` and accepts ISO strings on input.

```ts
/** Shape of a parsed event. */
export interface Event {
  /** Non-empty event name, e.g. "user.signup". */
  name: string;
  /** ISO-8601 timestamp, e.g. "2024-05-01T12:00:00.000Z". */
  timestamp: string;
  /** Arbitrary event data. */
  payload: Record<string, unknown>;
}

export class ParseEventError extends Error {
  constructor(message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = "ParseEventError";
  }
}

/**
 * Validates and normalizes an unknown value into an Event.
 * Throws ParseEventError if the input doesn't match the expected shape.
 */
export function parseEvent(raw: unknown): Event {
  if (typeof raw !== "object" || raw === null || Array.isArray(raw)) {
    throw new ParseEventError("Event must be a non-null object");
  }

  const candidate = raw as Record<string, unknown>;
  const { name, timestamp, payload } = candidate;

  if (typeof name !== "string" || name.trim() === "") {
    throw new ParseEventError("Event.name must be a non-empty string");
  }

  let iso: string;
  if (typeof timestamp === "string") {
    const parsed = Date.parse(timestamp);
    if (Number.isNaN(parsed)) {
      throw new ParseEventError("Event.timestamp must be a valid date string");
    }
    iso = new Date(parsed).toISOString();
  } else if (typeof timestamp === "number" && Number.isFinite(timestamp)) {
    iso = new Date(timestamp).toISOString();
  } else if (timestamp instanceof Date && !Number.isNaN(timestamp.getTime())) {
    iso = timestamp.toISOString();
  } else {
    throw new ParseEventError("Event.timestamp must be a string, number, or Date");
  }

  let data: Record<string, unknown>;
  if (payload === undefined) {
    data = {};
  } else if (typeof payload === "object" && payload !== null && !Array.isArray(payload)) {
    data = payload as Record<string, unknown>;
  } else {
    throw new ParseEventError("Event.payload must be an object");
  }

  return { name: name.trim(), timestamp: iso, payload: data };
}
```

Example:

```ts
const event = parseEvent({
  name: "user.signup",
  timestamp: "2024-05-01T12:00:00Z",
  payload: { userId: 42 },
});
// event: { name: "user.signup", timestamp: "2024-05-01T12:00:00.000Z", payload: { userId: 42 } }
```

Notes:
- `raw: unknown` forces callers to handle untrusted input; the function narrows and validates before constructing the `Event`.
- `timestamp` accepts an ISO string, epoch milliseconds, or a `Date`, and is always normalized to an ISO-8601 string.
- If you'd rather not throw, wrap it in a `Result` type (`{ ok: true; value: Event } | { ok: false; error: string }`) instead of using `ParseEventError`.

