## Notes / optional extras

- **JSON convenience:** if you want the function to serialize objects and set `Content-Type` automatically, widen the body type and add a branch:

  ```ts
  export interface RequestOptions {
    method?: HttpMethod;
    url: string;
    headers?: Record<string, string>;
    body?: BodyInit | Record<string, unknown> | null;
  }

  export async function request(options: RequestOptions): Promise<Response> {
    const { method = 'GET', url, headers = {}, body = null } = options;

    const isPlainObject =
      typeof body === 'object' &&
      body !== null &&
      !(body instanceof FormData) &&
      !(body instanceof Blob) &&
      !(body instanceof ArrayBuffer) &&
      !(body instanceof URLSearchParams) &&
      !(body instanceof ReadableStream);

    const finalBody =
      method === 'GET' || method === 'HEAD'
        ? null
        : isPlainObject
          ? JSON.stringify(body)
          : (body as BodyInit | null);

    const finalHeaders: Record<string, string> = {
      ...(isPlainObject ? { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } : {}),
      ...headers,
    };

    return fetch(url, { method, headers: finalHeaders, body: finalBody });
  }
  ```

- **Cancellation & timeouts:** add `signal?: AbortSignal` to the interface and pass it through to `fetch` — `AbortSignal.timeout(5000)` gives you a built-in timeout.
- **Non-2xx handling:** `fetch` only rejects on network errors, so check `res.ok` yourself (as above) if you want errors to throw.
- **Typing the response:** `Promise<Response>` is the honest return type; use `res.json()` and cast, or a generic wrapper like `request<T>(): Promise<T>` if you want the parsed payload typed.

