### Notes

- **Return type** is `Promise<Response>` — the raw fetch promise, so status codes, headers, and streaming bodies are all still available. The function is `async` only to give a clean `Promise<Response>` signature; it doesn't buffer or parse the body.
- **Body handling**: `body` is omitted entirely for `GET`/`HEAD`, since `fetch` throws a `TypeError` if you pass a body with those methods.
- **Headers**: a `Record<string, string>` is the simplest ergonomic choice. If you need repeated headers (e.g. multiple `Set-Cookie` values) or `Headers` objects, widen the type to `HeadersInit`.
- **JSON convenience**: if you want automatic serialization, add a variant that sets `Content-Type: application/json` and stringifies plain objects — but keep it separate so callers can send raw `FormData`, `Blob`, or `ReadableStream` bodies untouched.

```ts
// RequestOptions describes the inputs for a single HTTP request.
export interface RequestOptions {
  /** HTTP method, e.g. "GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE". Defaults to "GET". */
  method?: string;
  /** Target URL. */
  url: string;
  /** Optional request headers. */
  headers?: HeadersInit;
  /** Optional request body (ignored for GET/HEAD). */
  body?: BodyInit | null;
}

/**
 * Performs an HTTP request using the Fetch API.
 * @returns A Promise resolving to the Response.
 */
export function request(options: RequestOptions): Promise<Response> {
  const { method = "GET", url, headers, body } = options;

  // GET/HEAD requests must not include a body (fetch would throw).
  const init: RequestInit = { method, headers };
  if (body != null && method.toUpperCase() !== "GET" && method.toUpperCase() !== "HEAD") {
    init.body = body;
  }

  return fetch(url, init);
}
```

Example usage:

```ts
const res = await request({
  method: "POST",
  url: "https://api.example.com/items",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ name: "Widget" }),
});

if (res.ok) {
  const data = await res.json();
  console.log(data);
}
```

Notes:
- `RequestOptions` is declared as an exported `interface` so it can be reused and extended.
- `method` is optional and defaults to `"GET"`; `url` is the only required field.
- `HeadersInit` and `BodyInit` come from the DOM lib (`"dom"` in `tsconfig.json`); if you're in a Node environment, use `undici`'s types or define your own header/body types.

